index 00000000..8225c601
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ext/adr/0008.md
@@ -0,0 +1,17 @@
+# ADR 0008: Source-side indexed native Session pages
+
+## Decision
+
+The approved persistence extension adds optional `SessionPersistence.readPage` and `prepareReadIndex` capabilities. JSONL exposes them only with `indexedReading: true`; ordinary `open`, `read`, mutation, migration and execution ownership keep their existing behavior. A page contains an original contiguous body interval and its native turn, step, tool-call, assistant and request-header dependencies. Dependencies have separate coordinates and do not advance pagination. Admission covers the entire response after dependency closure. The reader reduces the body in the requested direction until that complete response fits; one indivisible item that cannot fit raises `SessionPageCapacityError` with `CAPACITY_EXCEEDED`.
+
+The source owns a derived SQLite sidecar containing native event coordinates, physical row/frame offsets and validation metadata. It does not store a second transcript. A worker streams the existing artifact once, preserving the installed vocabulary, physical framing and complete V4 relationship checks. It resumes the same validation state when the append-only source grows. A fixed physical prefix observed at operation start permits concurrent append without repeatedly waiting for the source to become idle. A source replacement, truncation or same-size revision rebuilds the derived index and assigns a new opaque generation UUID. Appends and process restarts retain that generation; native paths and inode metadata stay private to the sidecar. Each page holds a consistent SQLite read snapshot and limits dependency lookups to the accepted native prefix. SQLite transactions prevent publication of a partly indexed candidate; cancellation terminates the worker and leaves the prior committed index intact.
+
+Native V4 full restoration and incremental indexing share the same lifecycle validator. Its checkpoint retains surface sequence numbers, reference coordinates and unresolved execution metadata, rather than closed message bodies. Inherited delivery/catalog exemptions and orphan-compaction handling retain their released semantics. The original earlier event remains the authority for developer request headers and title references. No format version, committed generation or write lease is changed.
+
+## Cold and warm IO
+
+Operators may prepare indexes before enabling source reads with `prepareReadIndex`. An existing unindexed archive needs one source-side metadata/validation scan. That work remains explicit and cancellable and does not transfer the complete log into the display Host. Later page reads decode only their selected original rows or compressed frames and dependencies; appends index their new suffix. A Zstandard frame remains the smallest independently decodable physical unit. An old archive written as one very large frame therefore still requires that frame to be decoded for a page; this extension does not claim byte work proportional to the requested event count in that case.
+
+## Verification
+
+The native worker tests exercise actual plaintext and Zstandard files, dependency closure, complete-result admission, source prefix growth, torn-tail repair, required-vocabulary refusal and cancellation. IO observations assert that a warmed tail page does not scan the older source and that an append validates only newly added events. The V4 suites exercise the same release validation with checkpointed native tool lifecycles and inherited markers. Browser scroll, physical devices, installation and production acceptance remain separate checks owned by the consuming HHPI release.
